                <h2>Part 1: Account Setup - Don't Let "Default Settings" Trip You Up</h2>
<p>Pre-campaign account configuration is often overlooked, but Southeast Asia and Latin America have significantly different requirements from Western markets.</p>
<p><strong>Step 1: Time Zone and Currency Calibration</strong></p>
<p>Before building your first ad set, check your account time zone and billing currency. Set according to your target market's local time:</p>
<ul>
<li>Indonesia: GMT+7</li>
<li>Vietnam: GMT+7</li>
<li>Brazil: GMT-3 (note: Brazil observes daylight saving time)</li>
</ul>
<p>For LATAM, use local currencies (BRL, COP, MXN) rather than forcing USD, which creates unwanted FX perception for users.</p>
<p><strong>Step 2: Time-of-Day Testing</strong></p>
<p>Don't assume "global evening peak" applies everywhere. Use AOE testing for different dayparts and run at least 7 days before locking in your schedule.</p>
<h2>Part 2: Creative Localization: Translation ≠ Localization</h2>
<p>This is where 90% of new teams stumble.</p>
<p><strong>Step 3: Triple-Check Copy Localization</strong></p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Linguistic Accuracy</strong>: Use native speakers, not machine translation + human polish. Indonesian has complex formality levels; Portuguese distinguishes formal/informal "you."</li>
<li><strong>Cultural Fit</strong>: A utility app in Indonesia changed "quickly control your finances" to "easily manage every income" after religious users reacted negatively—CTR improved 23%.</li>
<li><strong>Localized CTAs</strong>: Southeast Asian users respond to "free claim" and "limited time offer"; LATAM users prefer "0% installment" messaging.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Step 4: Visual Asset Adaptation</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Ethnicity/Skin Tone: Brazilian audiences prefer diverse models; all-white imagery gets lower CTR</li>
<li>Religious Elements: Muslim-majority markets avoid alcohol, pork, revealing clothing</li>
<li>Color Preferences: Red/gold (prosperity, celebration) works well in Southeast Asia; green usage requires caution in some LATAM countries</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 5: Subtitles and Voiceovers</strong></p>
<p>When budget allows, localize core video assets. Vietnamese users show ~40% higher completion rates on Vietnamese-dubbed content versus subtitled versions. If budget is tight, at least ensure subtitles are correct and sized for mobile viewing.</p>
<h2>Part 3: Payments and Landing Pages: Hidden Conversion Killers</h2>
<p><strong>Step 6: Payment Method Checklist</strong></p>
<p>Payment preferences vary dramatically by market:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Market</th>
<th>Recommended Methods</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Indonesia</td>
<td>GoPay, OVO, DANA, bank transfer</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Vietnam</td>
<td>MoMo, VNPay, ZaloPay</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Brazil</td>
<td>Boleto Bancário, PIX, installments</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mexico</td>
<td>OXXO, Spei bank transfer</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>If your landing page doesn't support local主流 payment methods, conversion rates will crater.</p>
<p><strong>Step 7: Landing Page Language and Domain</strong></p>
<p>Don't redirect users to English pages after they click your ad. Use locally registered domains (.id, .vn, .br) for higher user trust. Payment flow language must remain consistent—half-English/half-Portuguese pages kill conversions.</p>
<h2>Part 4: Campaign Strategy - No "One-Size-Fits-All" Templates</h2>
<p><strong>Step 8: Localize Audience Targeting</strong></p>
<p>Don't copy North American interest combinations directly. Recommended approach:</p>
<ul>
<li>Start with Advantage+ auto-targeting for 3-5 days to establish baselines</li>
<li>Layer in locally popular interest terms (Indonesia: "shopee", "blibli"; Brazil: "encomendas")</li>
<li>Target by language, not "English"</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 9: Budget and Bidding Strategy</strong></p>
<p>CPMs in Southeast Asia are generally lower than Western markets, but competition in popular categories (gaming, e-commerce) is fierce. Start with lowest cost bidding, then switch to target cost after 3 days to control ROI.</p>
<h2>Part 5: Data Review - Validate Localization Results</h2>
<p><strong>Step 10: Cross-Market Metric Comparison</strong></p>
<p>Export country/language-segmented data weekly, focusing on:</p>
<ul>
<li>CTR: Is creative localization working?</li>
<li>CPC: Is audience targeting precise?</li>
<li>CVR: Are payments and landing pages smooth?</li>
<li>CPA: Is overall ROI hitting targets?</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Step 11: Localization Troubleshooting Checklist</strong></p>
<p>When a market's metrics significantly underperform the average, investigate in order:</p>
<ol>
<li>Account time zone/currency correct?</li>
<li>Creative language/culture obviously problematic?</li>
<li>Payment methods covering local主流 options?</li>
<li>Landing page load speed (Southeast Asia's 3G environment)?</li>
<li>Audience targeting language matching?</li>
</ol>
<h2>Final Reminder</h2>
<p>Localization isn't one-time work. Southeast Asia and LATAM holidays, cultural events, and marketing moments differ greatly from China and North America. Update your localization content calendar monthly, covering key dates like Ramadan/Eid, Independence Days, and Christmas shopping seasons.</p>
<p>Have specific localization challenges in your campaigns? Drop a comment below and we'll provide targeted advice.</p>
